{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And his eyes grew dim from seeing.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): dimming of the eyes is the way of old age, and all follows from 'and he grew old.' So too we find with Jacob (Genesis 48:10): ״ועיני ישראל כבדו מזוקן״, and with Ahijah the Shilonite (I Kings 14:4): ״כי קמו עיניו משיבו״; and what is written of Moses our teacher, peace be upon him — ״לא כהתה עינו ולא נס לחה״ — that was a miracle." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ותכהין עיניו מראות — they said in Bereshit Rabbah: because he took a bribe from Esau, his eyes grew dim, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:19): ״כי השחד יעור וגו׳״, and this is a warning to judges. Rabbi Yitzchak said: if one who took from one who owed him had his eyes grow dim, how much more so one who takes a bribe from one who does not owe him; and concerning this the verse says (Exodus 23:8): ״ושחד לא תקח וגו׳״. Another interpretation: so that Jacob should take the blessings. Another interpretation: because of the smoke of idolatry, since the wives of Esau would offer incense to idolatry. Another interpretation: so that he would not go outside and people would say of him, 'this is the father of a wicked man'; the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I will dim his eyes and he will sit in the house. And this is what is written (Proverbs 28:28): ״בקום רשעים יסתר אדם״. From here they said: whoever establishes a wicked son or a wicked disciple — his eyes grow dim. A wicked son — from Isaac, as it is written: ״ויהי כי זקן יצחק ותכהין עיניו מראות״; a wicked disciple — from Ahijah the Shilonite, who established Jeroboam, as it is written (I Kings 14:4): ״ואחיהו לא יכול לראות כי קמו עיניו משיבו״. Another interpretation: ותכהין עיניו מראות — from the Shekhinah, for at the time he was bound upon the altar he gazed at the Shekhinah, and it was decreed upon him then that his eyes would be dimmed; but the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to punish him immediately, for His intention was only to test Abraham. And this is why it says מראות and did not say מלראות — in the language of ״מראות אלהים״. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, drew a parable: to what may the matter be compared? To a king who was strolling at the entrance of his palace. He lifted his eyes and saw the son of his beloved gazing at him from the window. The king said: if I kill him now, I will crush my beloved; rather, I decree that all the windows be sealed. So too, when Isaac gazed at the Shekhinah at the time of the Binding, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: if I kill him now, I will crush Abraham My beloved; rather, I decree that his eyes be dimmed. This is what the verse says (Isaiah 5:23): ״מצדיקי רשע עקב שחד וצדקת צדיקים יסירו ממנו״ — because Isaac would justify the wicked Esau on account of the hunt in his mouth, therefore they would remove from him the righteousness of the righteous — this is Moses, of whom it is written (Deuteronomy 34:7): ״לא כהתה עינו ולא נס לחה וגו׳״." "<b>And he called Esau his elder son.</b> His father called him 'great' and his mother called him 'great,' as it is written: ״ותקח את בגדי עשו בנה הגדול״, but the Holy One, blessed be He, called him 'small,' as it is said (Obadiah 2): ״הנה קטן נתתיך בגוים״ — corresponding to 'a great slaughterer' (תורא טבחא): Esau the great is a great slaughterer, as it is said (Isaiah 34:6): ״כי זבח לה׳ בבצרה״, ״וטבח גדול בארץ אדום״; and Jacob, who was called 'small,' as it is said (Genesis 27): ״ותקרא ליעקב בנה הקטן״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, called him 'great,' as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:8): ״ומי גוי גדול״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:41.738067618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}